{
 "title": "NumPy Arrays, Step by Step",
 "mood": "happy",
 "steps": [
  {
   "narration": "A NumPy array is a grid of values you can calculate with all at once. Let’s build one in Python; its elements share a single data type.",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "1. Create an array",
     "size": "l",
     "color": "blue"
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "import numpy as np",
     "why": "Import NumPy with its usual nickname"
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "a = np.array([2, 4, 6])",
     "why": "Create a one-dimensional array"
    },
    {
     "kind": "grid",
     "caption": "a • three values in one dimension",
     "cells": [
      [
       "2",
       "4",
       "6"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "narration": "Every array has a shape and a data type. Shape tells you the size of each dimension; dtype tells you how its values are stored.",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "2. Inspect your array",
     "size": "l",
     "color": "blue"
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "a.shape # (3,)",
     "why": "One dimension, containing three elements"
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "a.ndim # 1",
     "why": "Number of dimensions"
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "a.dtype # an integer dtype",
     "why": "Exact integer width can depend on your system"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "narration": "Use square brackets to pick values, starting from index zero. A slice selects a range, stopping before its end index.",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "3. Select values",
     "size": "l",
     "color": "blue"
    },
    {
     "kind": "grid",
     "caption": "Positions start at zero",
     "cells": [
      [
       "index",
       "0",
       "1",
       "2"
      ],
      [
       "value",
       "2",
       "4",
       "6"
      ]
     ],
     "highlight": [
      1,
      1
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "a[0] # 2",
     "why": "First element"
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "a[1:3] # array([4, 6])",
     "why": "Include index 1; exclude index 3"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "narration": "Here’s the superpower: arithmetic works on every element without writing a loop. Multiplication with an asterisk is element by element, not matrix multiplication.",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "4. Calculate element by element",
     "size": "l",
     "color": "blue"
    },
    {
     "kind": "grid",
     "caption": "Same positions work together",
     "cells": [
      [
       "a",
       "2",
       "4",
       "6"
      ],
      [
       "a + 10",
       "12",
       "14",
       "16"
      ],
      [
       "a * 2",
       "4",
       "8",
       "12"
      ],
      [
       "a * a",
       "4",
       "16",
       "36"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "A single number broadcasts across the array.",
     "size": "m",
     "color": "pink"
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "a.sum() # 12",
     "why": "Reduce all elements to one total"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "narration": "Now arrange those same three values into each of two rows. This is a two-dimensional array: its shape gives rows first, then columns.",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "5. Add a second dimension",
     "size": "l",
     "color": "blue"
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "b = np.array([[2, 4, 6], [8, 10, 12]])",
     "why": "Nested lists become rows"
    },
    {
     "kind": "grid",
     "caption": "b.shape is (2, 3): 2 rows × 3 columns",
     "cells": [
      [
       "2",
       "4",
       "6"
      ],
      [
       "8",
       "10",
       "12"
      ]
     ],
     "highlight": [
      1,
      2
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "b[1, 2] # 12",
     "why": "Row index 1, column index 2"
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "b[:, 1] # array([4, 10])",
     "why": "All rows, column index 1"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "narration": "You can also keep only values that pass a test. The comparison builds a mask of True and False values, which selects matching elements.",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "6. Filter with a condition",
     "size": "l",
     "color": "blue"
    },
    {
     "kind": "grid",
     "caption": "Keep values greater than 3",
     "cells": [
      [
       "a",
       "2",
       "4",
       "6"
      ],
      [
       "a > 3",
       "False",
       "True",
       "True"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "math",
     "text": "a[a > 3] # array([4, 6])",
     "why": "Boolean indexing keeps the True positions"
    },
    {
     "kind": "note",
     "text": "Arrays organize values; indexing selects; arithmetic transforms; masks filter.",
     "size": "m",
     "color": "green"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
